#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:173
msgid "MapFile"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:175
msgid ""
"Structured text configuration files for MapServer applications. It "
"defines the field of the map and is used to tell MapServer where the data"
" is and where to output the image. It also defines the map layer. "
"Including their data source, projection and notation. It must have a .map"
" extension, otherwise MapServer will not recognize it. MapFile files "
"organize various map elements into a system of objects with hierarchical "
"relationships. Data sources, data formats used, user interaction and "
"support for the OGC protocol are also defined in MapFile. Objects "
"included in MapFile and their hierarchical relationships:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:186
msgid "Geographic Data"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:188
msgid ""
"MapServer can take advantage of many types of geographic information data"
" sources. The default is the ESRI data format, and other formats of data "
"are also supported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:192
msgid "HTMLPages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:194
msgid ""
"It is the interface between the user and MapServer, usually located at "
"the web root. In its simplest form, MapServer can be called to place a "
"static map image on an HTML page. To make the map interactive, the image "
"is placed on an HTML form on the page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:199
msgid "MapServer CGI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:201
msgid ""
"Binary executables. It can receive requests to return images, data, etc.,"
" located in the cgi-bin or scripts directory of the web server. The user "
"of the WEB server must have execute permissions on these directories. For"
" security reasons, they cannot be in the root directory of the web. By "
"default, this program is called mapserv."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:207
msgid "WEB/HTTP Server"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:209
msgid ""
"The HTML page is provided when the user’s browser is hit. You need a "
"working Web (HTTP) server. For example, Apache or Microsoft’s IIS, they "
"are on the same machine where you installed MapServer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:214
msgid "Two modes of MapServer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:216
msgid ""
"MapServer can be regarded as the general name of two independent modules:"
" MapServer CGI module and MapScript module. On the server side, any "
"module can be used to write WebGIS programs. However, they use the same "
"configuration file MapFile."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:221
msgid ""
"MapServer can run in two different modes: CGI and MapScript. In CGI mode,"
" MapServer runs as a CGI script in the Web server environment. This is "
"easy to set up and produce fast, direct applications. In MapScript mode, "
"you can access MapServer API from Perl,Python or PHP. The MapScript "
"interface allows flexible, feature-rich applications that can still take "
"advantage of MapServer’s template tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:229
msgid "Comparison between MapServer and GeoServer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:231
msgid ""
"MapServer and GeoServer are both widely used open source WebGIS tools. "
"Both publish maps through the Internet and are often compared. MAPSERVER "
"is also OGC-compliant WMS and non-transactional WFS, based on CGI and "
"written in C++; GEOSERVER is written in Java (you need to install Java "
"SDK (not JRE) to run it), based on servlet and using the struts "
"framework."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:238
msgid ""
"Functionally: MapServer is weaker than GeoServer, QGIS and stronger than "
"UDIG."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:240
msgid ""
"Efficiency: Mapserver’s support for WMS (Web Map service) is more "
"efficient, while Geoserver is better at attribute queries combined with "
"WFS (Web Feature service) specifications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:244
msgid ""
"In addition to functional comparison, special attention should be paid to"
" the technical selection during development. MapServer focuses on map "
"service functions and can be used as project components (or even core "
"components), but other functions need to be implemented by developers "
"most of the time; GeoServer is a relatively complete suite, which can "
"basically be used as a product after deployment and installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:251
msgid ""
"MapServer is not a full-featured GIS, it does not provide integrated DBMS"
" (Database Management System) tools, it has limited analytical "
"capabilities, and there are no georeferencing tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:255
msgid ""
"Which is better? It’s hard to tell, depending on how you want to publish "
"your data and what language you’re better at using. Both are excellent "
"and have excellent documentation and user base. GEOSERVER enables you to "
"edit spatial data online and generate thematic maps. Maps are published "
"in XML files. MAPSERVER is good at generating thematic maps and is more "
"mature than ever. Maps are published through text configuration files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:262
msgid ""
"Which one is more appropriate? I advocate using MapServer if you just "
"publish the map and don’t allow modification, it’s easier to maintain. "
"GeoServer was chosen because of wanting better features such as online "
"editing and database support like PostgreSQL or Oracle spatial database, "
"another advantage of GeoServer is that there is a free client software "
"UDIG."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:274
msgid ""
"Provides two ways of working, the CGI way (for CGI, AJAX, FLEX "
"developers) and the MapScript way (for Php, Java, C#, Python developers)."
" The native CGI method is the most efficient, and with TileCache, large-"
"scale map tile data can be quickly generated. Compared with commercial or"
" open source platforms based on .Net and J2EE, MapServer is more suitable"
" for high-load large-scale Internet map applications. MapServer is a map "
"service software written based on C, which is faster than GeoServer "
"written in JAVA. Moreover, the history of MapServer is longer than that "
"of GeoServer, and even the performance of MapServer is comparable to that"
" of commercial ArcIMS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:286
msgid "Characteristics of GeoServer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:288
msgid ""
"GeoServer（http://geoserver.org/ ）is a J2EE compliant and implements the "
"WCS, WMS and WFS specifications , supports TransactionWFS (WFS-T), and "
"its technical core is the integration of the well-known Java-developed "
"GIS tools GeoTools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:293
msgid ""
"For spatial information storage, it supports ESRI Shapefile and spatial "
"databases such as PostGIS, Oracle, and ArcSDE, and the output GML files "
"meet the requirements of GML2.1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:297
msgid ""
"Because it is pure Java, it is more suitable for complex environmental "
"requirements, and because of its open source, development organizations "
"can flexibly implement specific target requirements based on GeoServer, "
"which are lacking in commercial GIS components."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:302
msgid ""
"As a pure Java implementation, GeoServer is deployed in application "
"servers, such as Tomcat; its WMS and WFS components respond to requests "
"from browsers or uDig to access configured spatial databases, such as "
"PostGIS, OracleSpatial, etc., and generate Maps and GML documents are "
"transferred to the client."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:308
msgid "GeoServer has the following advantages:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:310
msgid ""
"Written in Java language, standard J2EE framework, based on servlet and "
"STRUTS framework, supporting efficient Spring framework development;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:313
msgid "Compatible with WMS and WFS features, support WFS-T specification;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:314
msgid "Efficient database supports PostGIS, ShapeFile, ArcSDE,Oracle, MySQL, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:316
msgid "Support hundreds of projections;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec01_mapserver/section.rst:317
msgid "Ability to output network maps to formats such as jpeg, gif, png;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:4
msgid "Install and configure MapServer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:6
msgid ""
"Setting up the initial runtime environment when starting a job can be "
"tedious, especially when following the step-by-step steps, but the basic "
"“Hello World” still doesn’t appear, and it can be maddening. Building a "
"running environment requires comprehensive technology, some experience, "
"and a bit of luck. Don’t underestimate the ability to configure the "
"runtime environment. Many programmers cannot build a development "
"environment by themselves, let alone a real production environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:15
msgid "Basic requirements for installing and configuring MapServer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:17
msgid ""
"MapServer is an open source software written in C language, which itself "
"relies on some open source or free libraries, such as Shapelib, FreeType,"
" Proj.4, GDAL/OGR, GD Library, Regex. MapServer is written in C, and the "
"sub-projects it depends on have corresponding C language implementation "
"versions. When running MapServer, the relevant class libraries must be "
"installed, and sometimes they also depend on specific versions of certain"
" class libraries. When compiling and installing MapServer, you need to "
"have a certain understanding of these libraries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:26
msgid ""
"Shapelib provides the ability to read, write and update data in the “ESRI"
" Shapefile” format, and to modify the corresponding property file (.dbf);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:29
msgid ""
"FreeType is a font rendering library, capable of rendering most vector "
"and bitmap font formats, designed to be small, efficient, highly "
"customizable, and as lightweight as possible without sacrificing "
"performance and functionality;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:33
msgid ""
"Proj.4 is a geographic projection library that provides a variety of "
"projection definitions and interfaces;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:35
msgid ""
"GDAL/OGR, GDAL (Geospatial Data Abstraction Library) is an open source "
"raster spatial data conversion library under the X/MIT license. It "
"utilizes an abstract data model to express the various file formats it "
"supports. It also has a range of command line tools for data "
"transformation and processing. OGR is a branch of the GDAL project, "
"similar in function to GDAL, except that it provides support for vector "
"data. There are many well-known GIS products that use the GDAL/OGR "
"library, including ESRI’s ArcGIS 9.3, Google Earth and the cross-platform"
" GRASS GIS system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:44
msgid ""
"GD Library, generate images dynamically, supports most formats: JPEG, "
"GIF, WEBP, XPM, BMP. Usually used to dynamically generate charts, "
"pictures, thumbnails, etc., often used in the web environment;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:47
msgid "Regex, provides regular expression support for MapServer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:49
msgid ""
"By understanding these libraries, you can simplify the installation "
"process by reducing the number of features to install. Even though you "
"only install a basic version of MapServer, it still has the ability to "
"create a powerful application. After you become familiar with MapServer, "
"you can add other features you want."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:55
msgid ""
"MapServer is a program that generates maps, but provides a CGI interface "
"(the operation of MapServer CGI depends on PHP), and related functions "
"can be called through Web access. So many times in order to run "
"MapServer, you need to install a web server (such as Apache 2), and a "
"tool (such as FastCGI) to let the application (MapServer) communicate "
"with the web server (Apache). Apache 2 does not have CGI enabled by "
"default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec02_setup/section.rst:66
msgid "Libraries that MapServer depends on"
msgstr ""

