msgid ""
"Objects and their hierarchical relationships in MapFile; note that some "
"objects are omitted Object for drawing convenience"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec07_mapfile-basic/section.rst:325
msgid ""
"The syntax of MapFile is simple. The keywords for MapFile include the "
"object name (all the objects in figure 2), the object keyword, and the "
"object closing tag “END”. Object names and object closing tag “END” are "
"case-insensitive; attribute names are case-sensitive Shapfile files that "
"configure ESRI should be uppercase, while PostGIS files are always "
"lowercase. In MapFile files, the symbol ``#`` is used for single-line "
"comments. MapFile files have a ``.map`` extension and can be written "
"using various text editors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec07_mapfile-basic/section.rst:334
msgid "Here is an example of MapFile:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec07_mapfile-basic/section.rst:336
msgid ""
"Configuring a complete MapFile file requires: GIS data source, font file,"
" symbol file. GIS data sources can be distributed on different computers:"
" The font file is used to define the type of font used in the output map."
" The point, line and face symbols used in the map can be defined in the "
"Symbol object in the MapFile file; However, in order to achieve symbol "
"reuse - without having to define the same symbols in different MapFile "
"files, a separate symbol file is usually defined outside the MapFile."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec07_mapfile-basic/section.rst:345
msgid ""
"Most of the objects in MapFile files either correspond to map elements, "
"or are commonly used in GIS. Such as Lengend,Scalebar,Layer, etc. The "
"function of these objects is obvious and easy to understand. The meaning "
"of the objects and attributes in the MapFile file will be explained in "
"detail in Appendix 1. Explain the meaning of four special objects here: "
"Web Object,Outputformat Object,Query Object,Reference Map Object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec07_mapfile-basic/section.rst:352
msgid ""
"Web Object defines how to handle the web interface. Such as: the "
"attribute IMAGEPATH defines the path to store temporary files and images,"
" and TEMPLATE defines the interactive interface file used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec07_mapfile-basic/section.rst:355
msgid ""
"Outputformate Object defines the format of the output image. MapServer "
"converts GIS data format files (such as shapefiles) into image formats "
"supported by the MIME (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions) protocol, "
"such as gif, png, jpeg, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec07_mapfile-basic/section.rst:359
msgid ""
"QueryMap Object defines the generation mechanism of query results. For "
"example: the attribute STYLE (not an object) sets how the selected "
"feature is displayed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec07_mapfile-basic/section.rst:362
msgid ""
"Reference Map Object defines how the reference map is created. MapServer "
"supports three reference map types. The most common use of a reference "
"map is as a map viewing window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../webgis-src/chb1_mapserver-setup/sec07_mapfile-basic/section.rst:366
msgid ""
"The query results can be observed using the reference map. When a point "
"query occurs, a dot symbol is generated in the output Reference Map to "
"indicate the location being queried. The area query generates a box in "
"the Reference Map to indicate the query area. Feature queries display the"
" features of the query in the Reference Map. In short, the Reference Map "
"can be regarded as the observation window of the query [5]."
msgstr ""

